## The idea

Keep the intervals **sorted by start, merged, and never touching**, in a `SortedList<int,int>` (start → end). Its Keys and Values can be read by position, so we can **binary search** for "the last interval starting at or before x". Adding finds that neighbour, then **swallows every interval that overlaps or touches** the new one. Removing cuts every overlapping interval and keeps the pieces left and right of the cut. A running total keeps CoveredLength O(1).

## Step by step

1. FloorIndex(x): binary search the Keys for the last start ≤ x (−1 if none).
2. Add(start, end): reject start ≥ end. Let i = FloorIndex(start). If that interval reaches start (its end ≥ start), the merged interval starts at its start; otherwise move i to the next interval.
3. While the interval at i starts ≤ the new end: extend the new end if needed, subtract its length from the total, remove it. Then insert the merged interval and add its length.
4. Remove(start, end): find the first interval that ends after start. While intervals start before end: remove each one, and remember the piece before start and the piece after end, if any. Put those pieces back.
5. Contains(p): i = FloorIndex(p); true if i ≥ 0 and p < end at i (half-open).
6. Intervals: copy the list into (Start, End) tuples, already sorted.

## Worked example

**Input:** Add [10,20), [30,40), [50,60), then Add [15,55)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| after three adds | {10→20, 30→40, 50→60}; covered 30 |
| Add(15,55): FloorIndex(15) | index 0 = [10,20), and 20 ≥ 15 → merged start = 10 |
| swallow [10,20) | end = max(55,20) = 55; covered 20 |
| swallow [30,40) | 30 ≤ 55; covered 10 |
| swallow [50,60) | 50 ≤ 55; end = 60; covered 0 |
| insert [10,60) | covered 50; Count 1 |

**Result:** one interval [10, 60), covered length 50

## Cost

Contains is O(log n). Add and Remove are O(log n) to find the spot plus the intervals they absorb. SortedList inserts/removes in the middle shift an array (O(n) worst case); appending in order — the perf test — is cheap. A balanced tree (SortedSet) removes that worst case at the cost of trickier code.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// Half-open intervals [start, end), kept merged: [1,3) + [3,5) = [1,5).
public sealed class IntervalSet {
    private readonly SortedList<int, int> _map = new SortedList<int, int>();   // start -> end
    private long _covered = 0;                                                 // running total

    public int Count {
        get { return _map.Count; }
    }

    public long CoveredLength {
        get { return _covered; }
    }

    public IReadOnlyList<(int Start, int End)> Intervals {
        get {
            var result = new List<(int Start, int End)>(_map.Count);
            for (int i = 0; i < _map.Count; i++) result.Add((_map.Keys[i], _map.Values[i]));
            return result;
        }
    }

    public void Add(int start, int end) {
        if (start >= end) throw new ArgumentException("start must be less than end");

        int newStart = start;
        int newEnd = end;

        // The interval just before (or at) start may touch us: if so, we start where it starts.
        int i = FloorIndex(start);
        if (i >= 0 && _map.Values[i] >= start) {
            newStart = _map.Keys[i];
        } else {
            i = i + 1;                       // it does not touch: begin with the next one
        }

        // Swallow every interval that overlaps or touches [newStart, newEnd).
        while (i < _map.Count && _map.Keys[i] <= newEnd) {
            int s = _map.Keys[i];
            int e = _map.Values[i];
            if (e > newEnd) newEnd = e;
            _covered -= e - s;
            _map.RemoveAt(i);                // the next interval slides into position i
        }

        _map.Add(newStart, newEnd);
        _covered += newEnd - newStart;
    }

    public void Remove(int start, int end) {
        if (start >= end) return;

        // First interval that could overlap: the floor of start, if it reaches past start.
        int i = FloorIndex(start);
        if (i < 0) i = 0;
        else if (_map.Values[i] <= start) i = i + 1;

        var keep = new List<(int Start, int End)>();
        while (i < _map.Count && _map.Keys[i] < end) {
            int s = _map.Keys[i];
            int e = _map.Values[i];
            _map.RemoveAt(i);
            _covered -= e - s;
            if (s < start) keep.Add((s, start));     // the part to the left of the cut
            if (e > end) keep.Add((end, e));         // the part to the right of the cut
        }

        foreach (var piece in keep) {
            _map.Add(piece.Start, piece.End);
            _covered += piece.End - piece.Start;
        }
    }

    public bool Contains(int point) {
        int i = FloorIndex(point);
        return i >= 0 && point < _map.Values[i];     // end is excluded
    }

    // Index of the last interval whose start <= x, or -1 if there is none.
    private int FloorIndex(int x) {
        int lo = 0;
        int hi = _map.Count - 1;
        int answer = -1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (_map.Keys[mid] <= x) {
                answer = mid;                 // a candidate; look for a later one
                lo = mid + 1;
            } else {
                hi = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return answer;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Touching intervals? Inverted? Duplicate add?**

Half-open → touching merges; reject inverted; idempotent

**Q: Brute force vs this?**

Re-sort list O(n log n) per add vs O(log n) amortised

**Q: Very large N?**

Still log n; memory is the limit

**Q: Concurrent writers and readers.**

One lock per op; volatile snapshot for CoveredLength

**Q: Millions of intervals, range-sum queries.**

Segment tree over compressed coordinates

**Q: How do you test it in CI?**

Property test vs a bitmap oracle

